\section{Representation of CAR}

Let $\mathcal{H}$ be a seperable Hilbert space and $a$ a linear map into the
bounded operators acting in a Hilbert space $\mathfrak{H}$
\begin{equation}
a:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathfrak{H}\right)
\end{equation}
satisfying
\begin{align}
a\left(  f\right)  a\left(  g\right)  +a\left(  g\right)  a\left(  f\right)
&  =\left\{  a\left(  f\right)  ,a\left(  g\right)  \right\}  =0\\
a\left(  f\right)  a\left(  g\right)  ^{\dagger}+a\left(  g\right)  ^{\dagger
}a\left(  f\right)   &  =\left\{  a\left(  f\right)  ,a\left(  g\right)
^{\dagger}\right\}  =\left\langle f\left\vert g\right.  \right\rangle
I_{\mathfrak{H}},
\end{align}
then $a$ is called a representation $\left\{  a,\mathfrak{H}\right\}  $ of the CAR.

$\lceil$If $\dim\left\{  \mathfrak{H}\right\}  =\infty$ then the $c^{\ast}%
$-algebra $\mathcal{A}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  $ generated by $\left\{
\left.  a\left(  f\right)  \right\vert f\in\mathcal{H}\right\}  $ is
isomorphic (is this true?) to the infinite tensor product, $%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigotimes }%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigotimes}
%EndExpansion
\left(  M\left(  2;\mathbb{C}\right)  \right)  $ of $M\left(  2;\mathbb{C}%
\right)  ,$ which is called the CAR\ algebra of $\mathcal{H}$ and
$\mathfrak{H=}%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigotimes }%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigotimes}
%EndExpansion
\left(  \mathbb{C}^{2}\right)  .\rfloor$

The representation $\left\{  a,%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigwedge }%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigwedge}
%EndExpansion
\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  \right\}  $ is called a Fock representation i.e.
in this case $\mathfrak{H=}%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigwedge }%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigwedge}
%EndExpansion
\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  $. There are many unitary inequivalent Fock representations.

$\lceil$I think it would be better if
\begin{equation}
a:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}%
\end{equation}
and $\mathcal{A}$ has nothing to do with $\mathfrak{H\rfloor}$

A state $\omega$ on $\mathcal{A}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  $ is determined by
the set of functions $\left\{  W_{n,m}\left(  f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots
g_{m}\right)  \right\}  $
\begin{equation}
\omega\left(  a\left(  g_{1}\right)  ^{\dagger}\cdots a\left(  g_{m}\right)
^{\dagger}a\left(  f_{1}\right)  \cdots a\left(  f_{n}\right)  \right)
=W_{n,m}\left(  f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right)  .
\end{equation}
The state $\omega$ produces a representation, $\pi_{\omega}$ via the GNS procedure.

Let $T\in\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  $ with $0\leq T\leq
I_{\mathcal{H}},$ so that
\begin{equation}
T=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\lambda_{j}P_{j},0\leq\lambda_{j}\leq1,
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  P_{j}\right\}  $ is a family of orthogonal projectors. Each
one of these projectors defines a subspace
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_{j}=P_{j}\mathcal{H},
\end{equation}
which can be used in the orthogonal decomposition
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigoplus \limits_{1\leq j\leq\infty}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq j\leq\infty}}
%EndExpansion
\mathcal{M}_{j}.
\end{equation}
A state $\omega$ is a product state relative to $\mathcal{M}_{j}$ if
\begin{equation}
\omega\left(  xy\right)  =\omega\left(  x\right)  \omega\left(  y\right)
\forall x\in\mathcal{M}_{j},\forall y\in\mathcal{M}_{k};1\leq j\neq
k\leq\infty.
\end{equation}
Define
\begin{equation}
W_{n,m}^{T}\left(  f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right)  =\delta
_{nm}\det\left\{  \left\langle \left.  Tf_{j}\right\vert g_{k}\right\rangle
\right\}
\end{equation}
then $\left\{  W_{n,m}^{T}\right\}  $ determines a state, $\omega_{T},$ on
$\mathcal{A}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  .$

If $T=0$ then the state is identical to the one given by the Fock vacuum
vector in the Fock representation, and gives rise to the Fock representation
$\pi_{\omega_{T}}.$

If $T=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}$ then $\omega_{T}$ is a tracial state on
$\mathcal{A}\left(  \mathcal{H}\right)  $ and the von Neumann algebra
generated by $\pi_{\omega_{T}}$ is isomorphic to $%
%TCIMACRO{\dbigotimes }%
%BeginExpansion
{\displaystyle\bigotimes}
%EndExpansion
\left(  M\left(  2;\mathbb{C}\right)  \right)  $ relative to the normalized trace.

If $T=\mu I_{\mathcal{H}},$ $0<\mu<\frac{1}{2},$the state $\omega_{T}$ gives
rise to a factor of Type III$_{\lambda}$, where
\begin{equation}
\lambda=\frac{\mu}{\left(  1-\mu\right)  }.
\end{equation}


The von Neumann algebra generated by the cyclic representation $\pi
_{\omega_{T}}$ is an AFD factor for any $T.$


